lessons.push({
  title: "Merge Sort and Divide and Conquer",
  minutes: 55,
  learn: ["Explain divide and conquer", "Write merge sort and the merge step", "Derive O(n log n) time and O(n) space", "Use the merge step to count inversions"],
  why: "Merge sort is the first O(n log n) algorithm most people learn, it is stable, its running time never degrades, and its idea (split, solve, combine) underlies many other algorithms.",
  realWorld: "Sorting very large files that do not fit in memory (external merge sort), merging sorted result lists from several servers.",
  simple: "Split the list in half, sort each half (the same way, recursively), then <strong>merge</strong> the two sorted halves by repeatedly taking the smaller front item.",
  detail: `<h3>Divide and conquer</h3>
<p>1) <strong>Divide</strong> the problem into smaller parts of the same kind; 2) <strong>conquer</strong> each part (recursively, with a base case of size 0 or 1); 3) <strong>combine</strong> the answers.</p>
<h3>Merge sort</h3>
<pre style="background:rgba(127,127,127,.14);padding:12px 14px;border-radius:8px"><code>def merge_sort(a):\n    if len(a) &lt;= 1:\n        return a\n    mid = len(a) // 2\n    return merge(merge_sort(a[:mid]), merge_sort(a[mid:]))</code></pre>
<p>The <code>merge</code> of two sorted lists uses two pointers and takes O(n) time.</p>
<h3>Why O(n log n)</h3>
<p>The list is halved log&#8322; n times (the depth of the recursion tree), and each level does O(n) work in total for merging: n × log n. This holds for best, average and worst cases. Extra memory for the merged lists is O(n) (plus O(log n) recursion stack).</p>
${table(["Property", "Merge sort"], [["Time", "&Theta;(n log n) always"], ["Space", "O(n)"], ["Stable", "yes (take from the left half when equal)"], ["In-place", "no (the usual array version)"], ["Good for", "linked lists, external sorting, guaranteed performance"]])}
<h3>Counting inversions</h3>
<p>While merging, when an item from the <em>right</em> half is taken before the remaining items of the left half, each of those remaining left items forms an inversion with it. Adding <code>len(left) - i</code> at that moment counts all inversions in O(n log n), much faster than the O(n²) pair check.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `def merge(left, right):\n    out, i, j = [], 0, 0\n    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):\n        if left[i] <= right[j]:\n            out.append(left[i]); i += 1\n        else:\n            out.append(right[j]); j += 1\n    return out + left[i:] + right[j:]`, note: "<= keeps the sort stable." },
  diagram: graph("Merge sort recursion tree", "A list of four numbers is split into halves, then single items, and merged back in sorted order level by level.", 640, 260,
    [{ id: "r", k: "proc", t: "[5, 2, 4, 1]", x: 320, y: 30, w: 130, h: 34 }, { id: "l", k: "proc", t: "[5, 2]", x: 180, y: 100, w: 90, h: 34 }, { id: "rr", k: "proc", t: "[4, 1]", x: 460, y: 100, w: 90, h: 34 }, { id: "a", k: "io", t: "[5]", x: 110, y: 170, w: 60, h: 32 }, { id: "b", k: "io", t: "[2]", x: 250, y: 170, w: 60, h: 32 }, { id: "c", k: "io", t: "[4]", x: 390, y: 170, w: 60, h: 32 }, { id: "d", k: "io", t: "[1]", x: 530, y: 170, w: 60, h: 32 }, { id: "m", k: "term", t: "merged: [1, 2, 4, 5]", x: 320, y: 238, w: 190, h: 34 }],
    [{ from: "r", to: "l" }, { from: "r", to: "rr" }, { from: "l", to: "a" }, { from: "l", to: "b" }, { from: "rr", to: "c" }, { from: "rr", to: "d" }]),
  example: { code: `def merge(left, right):\n    out, i, j = [], 0, 0\n    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):\n        if left[i] <= right[j]:\n            out.append(left[i])\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            out.append(right[j])\n            j += 1\n    return out + left[i:] + right[j:]\n\ndef merge_sort(a):\n    if len(a) <= 1:\n        return a\n    mid = len(a) // 2\n    return merge(merge_sort(a[:mid]), merge_sort(a[mid:]))\n\nprint(merge([1, 4, 9], [2, 3, 10, 11]))\nprint(merge_sort([5, 2, 4, 1, 3, 2]))\nprint(merge_sort([]), merge_sort([7]))\n\ndef sort_count(a):\n    if len(a) <= 1:\n        return a, 0\n    mid = len(a) // 2\n    left, x = sort_count(a[:mid])\n    right, y = sort_count(a[mid:])\n    out, i, j, cross = [], 0, 0, 0\n    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):\n        if left[i] <= right[j]:\n            out.append(left[i]); i += 1\n        else:\n            out.append(right[j]); j += 1\n            cross += len(left) - i\n    return out + left[i:] + right[j:], x + y + cross\nprint(sort_count([2, 4, 1, 3, 5]))\nprint(sort_count(list(range(2000, 0, -1)))[1])`, out: `[1, 2, 3, 4, 9, 10, 11]\n[1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n[] [7]\n([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 3)\n1999000` },
  lineByLine: [["merge(left, right)", "Two pointers walk the sorted halves, always taking the smaller front item."], ["out + left[i:] + right[j:]", "One list is exhausted; append whatever remains of the other."], ["merge_sort(a[:mid]) and a[mid:]", "Sort each half recursively."], ["cross += len(left) - i", "A right item jumps ahead of all remaining left items: that many inversions."], ["range(2000, 0, -1)", "A reversed list of 2000 items has 2000·1999/2 = 1,999,000 inversions, found in O(n log n)."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Merging <code>[1, 4, 9]</code> and <code>[2, 3]</code>:", headers: ["left front", "right front", "taken", "output so far"], rows: [["1", "2", "1", "[1]"], ["4", "2", "2", "[1, 2]"], ["4", "3", "3", "[1, 2, 3]"], ["4", "(empty)", "rest of left", "[1, 2, 3, 4, 9]"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "Merging sorted results from several sources with heapq.merge (a lazy k-way merge).", code: `import heapq\nserver_a = [1, 5, 9]\nserver_b = [2, 6]\nserver_c = [3, 4, 8]\nprint(list(heapq.merge(server_a, server_b, server_c)))`, out: `[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9]` },
  mistakes: ["Forgetting the base case.", "Using <code>&lt;</code> instead of <code>&lt;=</code> in the merge (breaks stability).", "Slicing creates copies: fine for learning, but note the extra memory.", "Not appending the leftover items.", "Assuming merge sort is in place."],
  practices: ["Know the merge step by heart: it is reused in many problems.", "Use <code>sorted()</code> in real code.", "Mention stability and O(n) extra space when asked."],
  interview: ["Explain merge sort and its complexity.", "Why is merge sort stable?", "How do you count inversions in O(n log n)?", "Merge sort vs quicksort?"],
  practiceQs: ["How many levels does the recursion tree have for n = 1024?", "Merge [2, 6] and [1, 3, 8] by hand.", "Why does merge sort not have a bad worst case?", "How does merging help external sorting?"],
  revision: ["Divide, conquer, combine.", "Merge of two sorted lists is O(n).", "Always &Theta;(n log n), O(n) space, stable.", "Inversions via the merge step."],
  hinglish: "Merge sort list ko do hisson mein todta hai, har hissa sort karta hai aur phir do sorted hisson ko jodta hai. Har level par O(n) kaam aur log n levels, isliye hamesha O(n log n). Merge ke dauran inversions bhi gine ja sakte hain.",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What is the time complexity of merge sort in the worst case?", ["O(n)", "O(n log n)", "O(n²)", "O(log n)"], 1, "Same in every case."),
    mcq("What is the extra space of the array version?", ["O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(n)", "O(n²)"], 2, "Merged lists need O(n)."),
    mcq("Which comparison in merge keeps the sort stable?", ["left <= right takes left first", "left < right takes right first", "Random", "Never compare"], 0, "Equal items keep their original order."),
    mcq("What is merge([1, 3], [2, 4])?", ["[1, 2, 3, 4]", "[1, 3, 2, 4]", "[1, 4, 2, 3]", "[4, 3, 2, 1]"], 0, "A sorted merge.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION"),
    mcq("What is merge_sort([]) ?", ["Error", "[]", "[0]", "None"], 1, "The base case returns the list as is.", "OUTPUT_PREDICTION")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Count inversions", "Read a line of integers. Print the number of inversions: pairs of positions i < j with a[i] > a[j]. Use merge sort so that it runs in O(n log n).", "nums = list(map(int, input().split()))\n# count inversions while merge-sorting\n", `import sys\nsys.setrecursionlimit(10000)\nnums = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\ndef sort_count(a):\n    if len(a) <= 1:\n        return a, 0\n    mid = len(a) // 2\n    left, x = sort_count(a[:mid])\n    right, y = sort_count(a[mid:])\n    out, i, j, cross = [], 0, 0, 0\n    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):\n        if left[i] <= right[j]:\n            out.append(left[i])\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            out.append(right[j])\n            j += 1\n            cross += len(left) - i\n    return out + left[i:] + right[j:], x + y + cross\n\nprint(sort_count(nums)[1])`, [["2 4 1 3 5", "3"], ["1 2 3", "0"]], [["2 4 1 3 5", "3"], ["1 2 3", "0"], ["3 2 1", "3"], ["5", "0"], ["1 1 1", "0"], ["8 4 2 1", "6"]])],
});

