lessons.push({
  title: "Quick Sort, Partitioning and Quickselect",
  minutes: 55,
  learn: ["Explain partitioning around a pivot", "Write quick sort and analyse best, average and worst cases", "Choose pivots to avoid the worst case", "Use quickselect to find the k-th smallest in O(n) average"],
  why: "Quick sort is the practical champion: in-place, cache friendly and fast on average. Its partition step also solves many interview problems (Dutch national flag, k-th element).",
  realWorld: "Many standard-library sorts in other languages are quick-sort based; selecting the median or top-k scores without sorting everything uses quickselect.",
  simple: "Pick a <strong>pivot</strong>. Move smaller items to its left and larger items to its right (partition). Then sort the left and right parts the same way.",
  detail: `<h3>Partition</h3>
<p>After partitioning, the pivot is in its <em>final</em> sorted position; everything to its left is smaller or equal, everything to its right larger or equal. Work: one pass, O(n).</p>
${table(["Case", "When", "Time"], [["Best / average", "pivot splits the data roughly in half", "O(n log n)"], ["Worst", "pivot is always the smallest or largest (e.g. first element on already sorted data)", "O(n²)"]])}
<p>Space: O(log n) stack on average, O(n) in the worst case. Quick sort is <strong>not stable</strong>. A <strong>random pivot</strong> or the <strong>median of three</strong> makes the worst case very unlikely.</p>
<h3>Three-way partition (Dutch national flag)</h3>
<p>For data with many duplicates, split into <code>&lt; pivot</code>, <code>== pivot</code>, <code>&gt; pivot</code>. The classic exercise sorts an array of 0s, 1s and 2s in one pass with three pointers (<code>low</code>, <code>mid</code>, <code>high</code>).</p>
<h3>Quickselect</h3>
<p>To find the k-th smallest, partition once and recurse into <em>only the side that contains position k</em>. Average O(n), worst O(n&sup2;). Python's <code>heapq.nsmallest</code> or <code>sorted(a)[k]</code> are simpler when performance is not critical.</p>
<h3>What Python uses</h3>
<p><code>sorted</code> and <code>list.sort</code> use <strong>Timsort</strong>: a stable hybrid of merge sort and insertion sort that exploits existing order and runs in O(n) on sorted data, O(n log n) worst case.</p>`,
  syntax: { code: `def quick_sort(a):\n    if len(a) <= 1:\n        return a\n    pivot = a[len(a) // 2]\n    return (quick_sort([x for x in a if x < pivot])\n            + [x for x in a if x == pivot]\n            + quick_sort([x for x in a if x > pivot]))`, note: "Readable version; in-place versions swap inside the list." },
  diagram: ((() => { const inner = arrayRow([7, 2, 9, 4, 6], 30, 40, { 4: AMBER }) + tx(120, 28, "pivot = 6 (last item)", 12) + arrayRow([2, 4, 6, 9, 7], 30, 130, { 2: GREEN }) + tx(125, 118, "after partition: smaller | pivot | larger", 12) + tx(125, 205, "the pivot 6 is now in its final position", 12); return svgWrap(280, 220, "Partitioning around a pivot", "The list 7 2 9 4 6 with pivot 6 becomes 2 4 6 9 7: smaller values left, the pivot in place, larger values right.", inner); })()),
  example: { code: `import random\n\ndef quick_sort(a):\n    if len(a) <= 1:\n        return a\n    pivot = a[len(a) // 2]\n    return (quick_sort([x for x in a if x < pivot])\n            + [x for x in a if x == pivot]\n            + quick_sort([x for x in a if x > pivot]))\n\ndef quickselect(a, k):\n    pivot = random.choice(a)\n    low = [x for x in a if x < pivot]\n    same = [x for x in a if x == pivot]\n    high = [x for x in a if x > pivot]\n    if k < len(low):\n        return quickselect(low, k)\n    if k < len(low) + len(same):\n        return pivot\n    return quickselect(high, k - len(low) - len(same))\n\ndef dutch_flag(a):\n    low, mid, high = 0, 0, len(a) - 1\n    while mid <= high:\n        if a[mid] == 0:\n            a[low], a[mid] = a[mid], a[low]\n            low += 1\n            mid += 1\n        elif a[mid] == 1:\n            mid += 1\n        else:\n            a[mid], a[high] = a[high], a[mid]\n            high -= 1\n    return a\n\nprint(quick_sort([7, 2, 9, 4, 6, 2]))\nprint(quickselect([7, 10, 4, 3, 20, 15], 2))\nprint(dutch_flag([2, 0, 2, 1, 1, 0]))\nrandom.seed(5)\ndata = [random.randint(0, 1000) for _ in range(500)]\nprint(quick_sort(data) == sorted(data))`, out: `[2, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9]\n7\n[0, 0, 1, 1, 2, 2]\nTrue` },
  lineByLine: [["pivot = a[len(a) // 2]", "The middle item avoids the sorted-input worst case of the first-item pivot."], ["three comprehensions", "Smaller, equal and larger parts: a three-way partition (clear, though not in place)."], ["quickselect(a, k)", "Zero-based k: only the part containing the answer is searched."], ["dutch_flag", "One pass: 0s are swapped to the front, 2s to the back, 1s stay in the middle."]],
  dryRun: { intro: "Quickselect for the 3rd smallest (k = 2) of <code>[7, 10, 4, 3, 20, 15]</code> with pivot 10:", headers: ["Part", "Values", "Decision"], rows: [["low (< 10)", "[7, 4, 3]", "3 items; k = 2 < 3, so recurse into low"], ["next: pivot 4", "low [3], same [4], high [7]", "k = 2 is past low(1)+same(1), so the answer is in high with k = 0 → 7"]] },
  realExample: { intro: "The median of a list without fully sorting, using quickselect.", code: `import random\ndef kth(a, k):\n    pivot = random.choice(a)\n    low = [x for x in a if x < pivot]\n    same = [x for x in a if x == pivot]\n    high = [x for x in a if x > pivot]\n    if k < len(low):\n        return kth(low, k)\n    if k < len(low) + len(same):\n        return pivot\n    return kth(high, k - len(low) - len(same))\nscores = [62, 90, 75, 88, 71]\nprint(kth(scores, len(scores) // 2), sorted(scores)[len(scores) // 2])`, out: `75 75` },
  mistakes: ["Choosing the first item as pivot on sorted data.", "Forgetting equal values (an endless recursion with two-way partitions on duplicates).", "Believing quick sort is stable.", "Recursing on both sides in quickselect.", "Mixing zero-based and one-based k."],
  practices: ["Randomise the pivot or use median of three.", "Use three-way partitioning with duplicates.", "Use Python's built-in sort in real code."],
  interview: ["What is the worst case of quick sort and how do you avoid it?", "Quick sort vs merge sort?", "How does quickselect work?", "Solve the Dutch national flag problem."],
  practiceQs: ["What is the pivot's position after partition?", "Why is the average O(n log n)?", "Sort 0s, 1s and 2s in one pass.", "Find the k-th largest instead of the smallest."],
  revision: ["Partition around a pivot; pivot lands in its final place.", "Average O(n log n), worst O(n²), not stable.", "Random or median-of-three pivot.", "Quickselect: O(n) average."],
  hinglish: "Quick sort pivot chunta hai, chhote ko left aur bade ko right bhejta hai (partition) aur dono hisson ko dohrata hai. Average O(n log n), kharab pivot par O(n²). k-th chhota nikalne ke liye sirf ek hissa dekhte hain (quickselect).",
  quiz: [
    mcq("What is the average time of quick sort?", ["O(n)", "O(n log n)", "O(n²)", "O(log n)"], 1, "Balanced splits."),
    mcq("What causes the worst case?", ["Random data", "Always picking the smallest or largest item as pivot", "Small lists", "Duplicates only"], 1, "Splits are 0 and n-1."),
    mcq("Is quick sort stable?", ["Yes", "No (the usual versions)", "Only on sorted data", "Only with duplicates"], 1, "Partitioning swaps distant items."),
    mcq("What sort does Python's list.sort() use?", ["Quick sort", "Merge sort only", "Timsort", "Bubble sort"], 2, "A stable hybrid of merge and insertion sort."),
    mcq("What does quickselect find?", ["The k-th smallest item", "The sorted list", "The median only", "Duplicates"], 0, "By recursing into one side.")
  ],
  tasks: [task("Sort 0s, 1s and 2s", "Read a line containing only the numbers 0, 1 and 2. Print them sorted in non-decreasing order, separated by spaces, using a one-pass three-pointer approach (Dutch national flag).", "a = list(map(int, input().split()))\n# one pass with three pointers\n", `a = list(map(int, input().split()))\nlow, mid, high = 0, 0, len(a) - 1\nwhile mid <= high:\n    if a[mid] == 0:\n        a[low], a[mid] = a[mid], a[low]\n        low += 1\n        mid += 1\n    elif a[mid] == 1:\n        mid += 1\n    else:\n        a[mid], a[high] = a[high], a[mid]\n        high -= 1\nprint(" ".join(map(str, a)))`, [["2 0 2 1 1 0", "0 0 1 1 2 2"], ["1 0", "0 1"]], [["2 0 2 1 1 0", "0 0 1 1 2 2"], ["1 0", "0 1"], ["2", "2"], ["0 0 0", "0 0 0"], ["2 2 1 1 0 0", "0 0 1 1 2 2"], ["1 2 0 1 2 0 1", "0 0 1 1 1 2 2"]])],
});

